window.CLATMOCK={"n":6,"title":"Mock Test 6","theme":"Constitutional law intensive","kind":"Revision paper","blurb":"Built from the 20 passages held back from Mocks 1, 2, 3 and 5: fifteen on constitutional law, with jurisprudence and environmental law. Ideal for the last stretch of revision.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"p":[{"id":"P01","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Freedom of Speech: Restrictions, Incitement and Courage of Courts","x":"Article 19(1)(a) guarantees to all citizens the freedom of speech and expression, and Article 19(2) permits the State to impose by law only reasonable restrictions in the interests of the sovereignty and integrity of India, the security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, or in relation to contempt of court, defamation or incitement to an offence. In Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015), the Supreme Court struck down Section 66A of the Information Technology Act as vague and overbroad, drawing the distinction between mere discussion or advocacy, which is protected however unpopular, and incitement, which can be restricted; the Court upheld Section 69A, which allows blocking of online content, because it was accompanied by procedural safeguards, including reasons recorded in writing and a review mechanism. In Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India (2020), the Court held that freedom of speech on the internet and the right to carry on trade through the internet enjoy constitutional protection, that an order suspending internet services must be published and is subject to judicial review on the proportionality test, and that indefinite shutdowns are impermissible. In Kaushal Kishor v. State of U.P. (2023), a Constitution Bench held that no restrictions beyond those enumerated in Article 19(2) can be imposed on the freedom of speech, and that fundamental rights under Articles 19 and 21 can, in certain circumstances, be enforced even against private persons. In Imran Pratapgarhi v. State of Gujarat (2025), the Court quashed an FIR registered over a poem posted on social media, observing that even if a large number of persons dislike the views expressed, the right of the speaker must be respected, that the effect of words must be judged by the standards of reasonable, strong-minded and courageous persons, not those of the weak and vacillating, and that the courts are duty bound to uphold the right.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a ground of restriction enumerated in Article 19(2) as set out in the passage?","o":["Public order","Public interest in general","Incitement to an offence","Decency or morality"],"a":1,"e":"'Public interest' at large is not a listed ground; Kaushal Kishor confirms that no additional restrictions are permitted.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Why was Section 66A struck down in Shreya Singhal?","o":["Because it lacked a review mechanism","Because it was vague and overbroad and impermissibly restricted discussion and advocacy","Because it applied only to journalists","Because it was passed by the wrong House"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the vagueness and overbreadth ground and the distinction between advocacy and incitement.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Section 69A was upheld in Shreya Singhal because","o":["it permitted unlimited blocking","it applied only to Government websites","it had no relation to Article 19","it had procedural safeguards such as written reasons and review"],"a":3,"e":"The safeguards saved the provision.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A district magistrate suspends internet services indefinitely and does not publish the order. On the basis of Anuradha Bhasin, the order is","o":["valid for up to one year","valid, as internet access is not a right","open to challenge, as indefinite shutdowns are impermissible and orders must be published and tested on proportionality","valid if the magistrate is satisfied"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states the three requirements: publication, proportionality review and no indefinite suspension.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The significance of Kaushal Kishor, as presented in the passage, is that it","o":["overrules Shreya Singhal","permits the State to add new grounds of restriction under Article 21","holds that restrictions on speech are limited to those in Article 19(2) and that certain fundamental rights can be enforced against private persons","abolishes the right to speech against private persons"],"a":2,"e":"This captures both limbs of the holding in the passage.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The Court in Imran Pratapgarhi suggests that the effect of speech is to be judged by the standard of","o":["the most sensitive member of the audience","reasonable, strong-minded and courageous persons","the police officer registering the case","the majority of the public"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the standard.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P02","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Property under Article 300A and Compulsory Acquisition","x":"The Constitution (44th Amendment) Act, 1978 omitted the right to property from the list of fundamental rights, repealing Article 19(1)(f) and Article 31, and inserted Article 300A, which provides that no person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law. The right to property thus ceased to be a fundamental right but remains a constitutional right. In Jilubhai Nanbhai Khachar v. State of Gujarat (1995), the Supreme Court held that Article 300A requires the deprivation to be by a valid law, and that the 'law' must be one which satisfies the standards of reasonableness. In K.T. Plantation Pvt. Ltd. v. State of Karnataka (2011), the Court held that a law depriving a person of property must serve a public purpose, though the adequacy of compensation is not justiciable and the absence of a provision for compensation does not by itself invalidate the law; in certain cases, however, it may be necessary under Article 300A. In Vidya Devi v. State of Himachal Pradesh (2020), the Court described the right to property as a human right and a constitutional right, and held that the State cannot take possession of private land without the authority of law, nor claim title by adverse possession against a citizen. In Kolkata Municipal Corporation v. Bimal Kumar Shaw (2024), a seven-judge Bench held that Article 300A contains certain procedural rights of the owner, which the State must observe before depriving him of property: the right to notice, the right to be heard, the right to a reasoned decision, the duty to acquire only for a public purpose, the right to fair compensation, the right to an efficient and expeditious process, and the right of conclusion, namely that the acquisition must be completed. The Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013 (LARR Act) replaced the Land Acquisition Act, 1894. Under Section 24(2), an acquisition initiated under the old Act lapses where, in the case of land acquisition proceedings, the award was made five years or more before the commencement of the 2013 Act, but physical possession was not taken or compensation was not paid. In Indore Development Authority v. Manohar Lal (2020), a Constitution Bench held that compensation is 'paid' where it is tendered, and that the deposit of compensation in the treasury is not by itself sufficient for the owner to be deprived; and that the lapse requires both failures, possession and payment, together.","q":[{"q":"After the 44th Amendment, the right to property is","o":["a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(f)","a Directive Principle","abolished","a constitutional right under Article 300A, though not a fundamental right"],"a":3,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In K.T. Plantation, the Supreme Court held that","o":["a law depriving property must serve a public purpose, though the adequacy of compensation is not justiciable","compensation must always be adequate","the right to property is a fundamental right","property can be taken without any law"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records this holding.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the procedural rights recognised in Bimal Kumar Shaw?","o":["Right to a reasoned decision","Right to select the acquiring authority","Right to notice","Right to be heard"],"a":1,"e":"The last is not among the seven rights.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under Section 24(2) of the LARR Act, as interpreted in Indore Development Authority, an acquisition lapses where","o":["either possession was not taken or compensation was not paid","both physical possession was not taken and compensation was not paid, for the requisite period","compensation was deposited in the treasury","the owner objected"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that the lapse requires both failures.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Compensation is 'paid' within Section 24(2) when","o":["it is announced in the award","it is deposited in the treasury","it is entered in the Government's accounts","it is tendered to the owner"],"a":3,"e":"The Constitution Bench held that tender is sufficient for 'payment'; treasury deposit alone is not.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Vidya Devi, the Court held that the State","o":["has absolute power over land","may claim title to private land by adverse possession","cannot take possession of private land without the authority of law, nor claim title by adverse possession against a citizen","may deprive land without notice"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states this.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P03","s":"Environmental Law","sf":"Environmental Law","t":"Pollution Control Statutes and the National Green Tribunal","x":"The Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 and the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 create the Central and State Pollution Control Boards, which grant consent to establish and operate industries and may issue closure directions. Parliament has since decriminalised many offences. The Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act, 2023 replaced imprisonment for contravention of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 with a monetary penalty of not less than Rs 10,000, extendable to Rs 15 lakh, imposed by an adjudicating officer, and the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Amendment Act, 2024 adopted a similar approach. Critics fear that deterrence has been weakened, while the Government regards the change as a way of reducing the burden of litigation. The National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 established the Tribunal for the effective and expeditious disposal of cases relating to environmental protection. Under Section 14, the Tribunal hears civil cases involving a substantial question relating to the environment which arise out of the enactments listed in Schedule I: the Water Act, the Water Cess Act, the Forest (Conservation) Act, the Air Act, the Environment (Protection) Act, the Public Liability Insurance Act and the Biological Diversity Act. The Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 and the Indian Forest Act, 1927 are not in the Schedule. An application under Section 14 must be made within six months of the cause of action, extendable by up to sixty days, whereas an application for compensation under Section 15 may be made within five years. Under Section 20, the Tribunal must apply the principles of sustainable development, the precautionary principle and the polluter pays principle. It is not bound by the Code of Civil Procedure or the law of evidence, but is guided by principles of natural justice, and an appeal against its orders lies to the Supreme Court within ninety days (Section 22). In Municipal Corporation of Greater Mumbai v. Ankita Sinha (2021), the Supreme Court held that the Tribunal may take cognisance of environmental issues suo motu, since its role is wider than that of a body deciding only disputes between parties.","q":[{"q":"An appeal against an order of the National Green Tribunal lies to the Supreme Court within","o":["thirty days","sixty days","one year","ninety days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 22 provides for an appeal within ninety days.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following enactments is NOT listed in Schedule I to the National Green Tribunal Act, as described in the passage?","o":["The Indian Forest Act, 1927","The Biological Diversity Act, 2002","The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981","The Public Liability Insurance Act, 1991"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that the Wild Life (Protection) Act and the Indian Forest Act are outside the Schedule.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The effect of the Jan Vishwas Act, 2023 on the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986, as described in the passage, was to","o":["transfer all environmental offences to the Supreme Court","increase the term of imprisonment for contravention","abolish the Pollution Control Boards","replace imprisonment for contravention with a monetary penalty of Rs 10,000 to Rs 15 lakh imposed by an adjudicating officer"],"a":3,"e":"The passage records this change.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Ankita Sinha, the Supreme Court held that the National Green Tribunal","o":["has no power to act except on the application of a party","is bound strictly by the Code of Civil Procedure","may decide only criminal matters","may take cognisance of environmental issues suo motu"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states this holding.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Residents discover on 1 January 2022 that a factory has contaminated their groundwater. On 1 December 2024 they apply to the Tribunal for compensation under Section 15. On the passage, the application is","o":["barred, since appeals lie only within ninety days","maintainable only before a civil court","barred, since it was not made within six months and sixty days","within the time allowed, since compensation applications may be made within five years"],"a":3,"e":"Section 15 allows five years; the six-month period applies to Section 14 applications.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Consider the following statements. 1. The National Green Tribunal is bound strictly by the Code of Civil Procedure and the law of evidence. 2. The Tribunal must apply the principles of sustainable development, precaution and polluter pays. 3. The Supreme Court has recognised the Tribunal's power to act suo motu. Which of the statements are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"e":"Statements 2 and 3 are correct; the Tribunal is guided by natural justice and is not strictly bound by the Code or evidence law.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P04","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Money Bills and Ordinances: Articles 109, 110 and 123","x":"A Money Bill, defined in Article 110, deals only with matters such as taxes, borrowing and the Consolidated Fund of India. It can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha on the recommendation of the President. After it is passed, it is transmitted to the Rajya Sabha, which must return it within fourteen days with recommendations; the Lok Sabha may accept or reject them, and if the Rajya Sabha does not return the Bill within fourteen days it is deemed to have been passed by both Houses. The Speaker's certificate that a Bill is a Money Bill is made final by Article 110(3), and the provisions for a joint sitting in Article 108 do not apply to Money Bills or Constitution Amendment Bills. In K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (the Aadhaar case, 2018), the majority upheld the Aadhaar Act as a Money Bill, while Justice D.Y. Chandrachud dissented, holding that the route was a fraud on the Constitution because it bypassed the Rajya Sabha. Article 123 empowers the President, at any time except when both Houses are in session, to promulgate an Ordinance when satisfied that immediate action is necessary. An Ordinance has the same force as an Act of Parliament, must be laid before both Houses, and ceases to operate at the expiration of six weeks from the reassembly of Parliament, or earlier if both Houses pass resolutions disapproving it. An Ordinance cannot amend the Constitution. In D.C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar (1987), the Court held that the repeated re-promulgation of Ordinances without placing them before the Legislature is a fraud on the Constitution, a view affirmed by a seven-judge Bench in Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar (2017), which also held that rights and liabilities that arose during the life of a lapsed Ordinance do not automatically vanish. Article 213 confers a similar power on Governors.","q":[{"q":"The Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill to the Lok Sabha within","o":["30 days","6 weeks","6 months","14 days"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill within fourteen days, failing which it is deemed passed.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The joint sitting under Article 108 cannot be used for","o":["Ordinary Bills passed by the Lok Sabha","any Bill rejected by the Rajya Sabha","Money Bills and Constitution Amendment Bills","Bills introduced in the Rajya Sabha"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that Article 108 does not apply to Money Bills or Constitution Amendment Bills.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In the Aadhaar case, Justice Chandrachud's dissent held that","o":["the Speaker's certificate under Article 110(3) is conclusive and unreviewable by courts","Money Bills may validly be introduced in the Rajya Sabha as well","the Money Bill route bypassed the Rajya Sabha and was a fraud on the Constitution","the Aadhaar Act violated only the freedom under Article 19"],"a":2,"e":"The passage records his view that the Money Bill route bypassed the Rajya Sabha and was a fraud on the Constitution.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"An Ordinance is promulgated while Parliament is in recess, and Parliament reassembles on 1 March. Assuming no disapproving resolutions, the Ordinance ceases to operate on","o":["1 May","12 April","1 April","31 March"],"a":1,"e":"It ceases six weeks (42 days) after reassembly: 1 March plus 42 days is 12 April.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A State Government has the Governor promulgate the same Ordinance four times in succession without placing it before the Legislature. Under D.C. Wadhwa, this practice is","o":["valid, so long as the Governor is satisfied each time","valid for six months only","valid if the Council of Ministers approves each re-promulgation","a fraud on the Constitution and invalid"],"a":3,"e":"Wadhwa held that repeated re-promulgation without placing the Ordinance before the Legislature is a fraud on the Constitution.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Only one House of Parliament is in session. On Article 123 as described, the President","o":["may promulgate an Ordinance only after first summoning the other House","may promulgate an Ordinance only on a request made by the Speaker","may not promulgate an Ordinance, since one House is in session","may promulgate an Ordinance, as the bar applies only when both Houses sit"],"a":3,"e":"Article 123 bars an Ordinance only when both Houses are in session.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P05","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law / Administrative Law","t":"'Bulldozer Justice' and Due Process","x":"In recent years, several State authorities have demolished the homes and properties of persons accused of crimes, often without prior notice and on the allegation that the structures were unauthorised. In In Re: Directions in the matter of demolition of structures (November 2024), the Supreme Court condemned the practice. It held that the executive cannot act as a judge and determine the guilt of an accused, and then punish him by demolishing his home; such action violates the separation of powers and the right to shelter, which is part of the right to life under Article 21. Reiterating that punishing the family of the accused for the alleged acts of one member is unconstitutional, the Court laid down binding, pan-India directions under Article 142. No demolition may proceed without a show-cause notice served by registered post and affixed on the structure, giving at least fifteen days' time or the time required by the local municipal law, whichever is longer. The notice must specify the nature of the unauthorised construction, the particular violation and the grounds for demolition, and must provide an opportunity of a personal hearing. The final order must be reasoned and communicated, and the owner must be given fifteen days to challenge it or remove the structure voluntarily before any demolition. The process must be videographed and notified on a digital portal. Officials who violate the directions are liable for contempt and may be personally required to restore the property at their own cost and pay compensation. The Court clarified that the directions do not apply to unauthorised structures in public places such as roads, streets, footpaths, railway lines or water bodies, or to cases where a court has itself ordered demolition.","q":[{"q":"The principal constitutional objection of the Court to demolition as punishment was that it","o":["is permitted if the accused is convicted","violates Article 19(1)(g) alone","violates the separation of powers and the right to shelter under Article 21","violates only municipal law"],"a":2,"e":"The passage records both separation of powers and Article 21 as the basis.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT among the procedural safeguards mentioned in the passage?","o":["A personal hearing","Prior sanction of the High Court for each demolition","A reasoned final order with fifteen days to challenge or remove the structure","A show-cause notice by registered post and affixed on the structure"],"a":1,"e":"The High Court's prior sanction is not required; the other three safeguards are listed.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A municipality wishes to remove a hut built on a railway track. According to the passage, the directions","o":["apply only if the occupant has a criminal record","apply only if the hut is more than ten years old","do not apply to unauthorised structures on railway lines","apply fully and the municipality must follow every step"],"a":2,"e":"Public places such as railway lines are specifically carved out.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Court's reference to the family of an accused being punished suggests that","o":["punishing relatives for the acts of an accused is unconstitutional","the family's liability depends on whether it owns the house","collective punishment is acceptable in grave cases","the Court has no view on the matter"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records that such punishment of the family is unconstitutional.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"An officer demolishes a house without notice, in breach of the directions. Which consequence is indicated in the passage?","o":["The officer may be liable for contempt and be required to restore the property at personal cost and pay compensation","Only the State Government can be held liable","The officer has immunity as he acted in good faith","The owner may only seek a fresh notice"],"a":0,"e":"Personal liability, contempt and restoration at the officer's cost are specified.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following best captures the constitutional principle on which the decision rests?","o":["Article 142 allows the Court to abolish municipal law","Adjudication of guilt and imposition of punishment belong to the judiciary, and State action affecting shelter must follow fair, reasoned procedure","Demolition is a civil remedy and needs no safeguards","Administrative expediency prevails over procedure in cases of crime"],"a":1,"e":"The decision is rooted in separation of powers and due process under Article 21.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P06","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law / Election Law","t":"Political Funding, the Voter's Right to Know and Electoral Disqualification","x":"The right to vote is a statutory right, but the right to information about candidates and political funding has been derived from Article 19(1)(a). In Union of India v. Association for Democratic Reforms (2002) and PUCL v. Union of India (2003), the Supreme Court held that voters have a fundamental right to know the criminal, financial and educational antecedents of candidates, since an informed choice is essential to democracy. In PUCL v. Union of India (2013), the Court directed the Election Commission to provide the option 'None of the Above' (NOTA) on voting machines, as an expression of the negative vote under Article 19(1)(a), though a NOTA majority does not, under the present law, result in a fresh election. In Lily Thomas v. Union of India (2013), the Court struck down Section 8(4) of the Representation of the People Act, 1951, which had allowed a convicted legislator to continue in office pending appeal, so that a legislator convicted of an offence carrying a sentence of two years or more is disqualified on the date of conviction. In Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India (February 2024), a Constitution Bench held unanimously that the Electoral Bond Scheme was unconstitutional. The Scheme allowed donors to purchase bonds from the State Bank of India and give them to political parties, without disclosing the donor's name to the public. The Court held that political contributions are linked with political choices and that voters have a right to information about funding to prevent 'quid pro quo' arrangements; that anonymity could not be justified by the donor's right to privacy, since a less restrictive means was available, which would serve the purpose of curbing black money; and that the related amendments to the Representation of the People Act, the Income-tax Act and the Companies Act, including the removal of the cap on corporate donations and of the requirement to disclose party-wise contributions, were unconstitutional. The Court directed the State Bank to disclose the details of purchases and redemptions to the Election Commission for publication.","q":[{"q":"The voter's right to know the antecedents of candidates was derived by the Supreme Court from","o":["Article 324","Article 19(1)(a)","Article 326 alone","Article 32"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that the right derives from Article 19(1)(a).","d":"Easy"},{"q":"What is the effect of a NOTA majority under the law as described in the passage?","o":["It does not result in a fresh election; it expresses a negative vote","The candidate with the next highest votes is declared elected","The election is countermanded","A fresh election is held"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that NOTA does not lead to a fresh election.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"After Lily Thomas, a legislator convicted of an offence and sentenced to two years or more is","o":["disqualified only after the appeal is dismissed","disqualified only after six years","entitled to continue in office pending appeal","disqualified from the date of conviction"],"a":3,"e":"Section 8(4), which protected such legislators, was struck down.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following was NOT a ground relied upon by the Court in holding the Electoral Bond Scheme unconstitutional?","o":["The Scheme violated Article 25","Existence of a less restrictive means to curb black money","The possibility of quid pro quo arrangements","The voter's right to information about political funding"],"a":0,"e":"Article 25 was not part of the reasoning.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The Court's reasoning on donor anonymity can best be described as that","o":["donor privacy is absolute","only corporate donors are protected","privacy of the donor cannot justify anonymity because a less restrictive means to serve the purpose existed, and the voter's right to know outweighs it","donors have no privacy rights"],"a":2,"e":"This is the proportionality-type reasoning stated in the passage.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. The Court directed disclosure of the details of bond purchases and redemptions to the Election Commission. 2. The Court upheld the removal of the cap on corporate donations. 3. The decision was by a Constitution Bench. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"The Court struck down the removal of the cap; statements 1 and 3 are correct.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P07","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law / Environmental Law","t":"Forest Rights, Gram Sabhas and Sustainable Livelihoods","x":"The Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006 (FRA) seeks to undo what it calls the historical injustice done to forest-dwelling communities. It recognises individual rights to hold and live in forest land under occupation, up to a ceiling of four hectares, and community rights, including the right to protect, regenerate, conserve or manage any community forest resource (Section 3(1)(i)). The Gram Sabha is the authority to initiate the process of determining rights (Section 6), assisted by a Forest Rights Committee, with appeals to the Sub-Divisional and District Level Committees. At least one-third of the members of the Committee must be women, and titles are to be registered jointly in the names of both spouses or, for a household headed by a single person, in the name of that person. Section 4(5) provides that no member of a forest-dwelling community shall be evicted or removed from forest land until the recognition and verification procedure is complete. Section 5 empowers the holders of rights and Gram Sabhas to protect wildlife, forests and biodiversity. In Samatha v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1997), the Supreme Court held that, in Scheduled Areas, the lease of tribal land to non-tribals for mining is void and that the State cannot grant mining leases to private persons, such activity being reserved to tribals, their cooperatives or State agencies. In Orissa Mining Corporation v. Ministry of Environment and Forests (the Niyamgiri case, 2013), the Court directed that Gram Sabhas decide whether the religious and cultural rights of the Dongria Kondh would be affected by bauxite mining, and the Gram Sabhas rejected the project. In 2022, the Forest (Conservation) Rules allowed the settlement of rights under the FRA to be completed after the in-principle approval of a forest diversion proposal, a change criticised as weakening community consent. The right to livelihood under Article 21, the Fifth Schedule and the Panchayats (Extension to Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996 reinforce the position of Gram Sabhas.","q":[{"q":"Under the FRA, the authority to initiate the process of determining forest rights is","o":["the Divisional Forest Officer","the National Green Tribunal","the District Collector alone","the Gram Sabha"],"a":3,"e":"Section 6 gives this role to the Gram Sabha.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In the Niyamgiri case, the Supreme Court directed that","o":["Gram Sabhas decide whether the religious and cultural rights of the Dongria Kondh would be affected by the mining","the High Court appoint a committee of experts to decide all questions","the mining project be approved without further inquiry","the Central Government alone decide on the mining project"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records this direction.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"As regards the registration of titles under the FRA, the passage states that titles are to be","o":["registered only in the name of the Gram Sabha","registered jointly in the names of both spouses, or in the name of the single head of a household","registered in the name of the male head of the household only","registered in the name of the Forest Department"],"a":1,"e":"The passage describes joint titles, which also protect women's claims.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Section 4(5) of the FRA provides that no forest dweller shall be evicted from forest land","o":["until the recognition and verification procedure is complete","unless the Forest Department so decides","unless the occupation exceeds four hectares","without the order of the Supreme Court in every case"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the protection.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A State grants a mining lease over tribal land in a Scheduled Area to a private company. On the basis of Samatha, the lease is","o":["valid if the Gram Sabha is informed afterwards","valid, since the State owns all minerals","valid if the company pays compensation","void, since in Scheduled Areas such mining is reserved to tribals, their cooperatives or State agencies"],"a":3,"e":"Samatha held such leases to private persons void.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Assertion (A): The FRA treats forest-dwelling communities not only as holders of rights but also as custodians of forests and biodiversity. Reason (R): Section 5 empowers the holders of forest rights and Gram Sabhas to protect wildlife, forests and biodiversity. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Section 5 gives statutory expression to the custodial role asserted in A.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P08","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Elections and the Constitution: the 61st Amendment, Article 324 and Disqualification","x":"The Constitution (Sixty-first Amendment) Act, 1988 reduced the voting age under Article 326 from twenty-one to eighteen years. Article 324 vests the superintendence, direction and control of elections in the Election Commission. The Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) can be removed only in the manner and on the grounds applicable to a Supreme Court judge, while other Election Commissioners can be removed only on the CEC's recommendation (Article 324(5)). Article 329(b) provides that no election to Parliament or a State Legislature shall be called in question except by an election petition. In Mohinder Singh Gill v. Chief Election Commissioner (1978), the Court treated Article 324 as a reservoir of power to act where no law exists. In Anoop Baranwal v. Union of India (2023), a Constitution Bench held that until Parliament makes a law, the CEC and Election Commissioners shall be appointed by the President on the advice of a committee of the Prime Minister, the Leader of the Opposition and the Chief Justice of India. Parliament later enacted the Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners (Appointment, Conditions of Service and Term of Office) Act, 2023, replacing the Chief Justice with a Union Cabinet Minister nominated by the Prime Minister; the Act has been challenged, and its current status should be verified before it is cited. Under Section 8(3) of the Representation of the People Act, 1951, a person convicted and sentenced to imprisonment of two years or more is disqualified from the date of conviction and for six years after release. In Lily Thomas v. Union of India (2013), the Court struck down Section 8(4), which allowed sitting legislators to continue pending appeal. Disqualification is not incurred if the appellate court stays the conviction itself, not merely the sentence (Lok Prahari, 2018). In Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India (2024), the Court struck down the Electoral Bonds Scheme as violating the voter's right to information under Article 19(1)(a).","q":[{"q":"The 61st Amendment, 1988 reduced the voting age under Article 326 to","o":["eighteen years","twenty-one years","nineteen years","twenty years"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that the voting age was reduced from twenty-one to eighteen.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A challenge to the validity of an election to the Lok Sabha must, under Article 329(b), be made by","o":["a writ petition directly under Article 32","a representation to the Election Commission","an election petition","a civil suit in a District Court"],"a":2,"e":"Article 329(b) permits an election to be questioned only by an election petition.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Under Article 324(5), an Election Commissioner other than the CEC can be removed","o":["only on the recommendation of the CEC","at the pleasure of the President","only in the manner and on the grounds applicable to a Supreme Court judge","by the Prime Minister on the advice of the Leader of the Opposition"],"a":0,"e":"Only the CEC enjoys Supreme Court judge-like protection; other Commissioners are removable on the CEC's recommendation.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A sitting Member of Parliament is convicted and sentenced to three years' imprisonment. His appeal is pending, and the appellate court stays only the sentence, not the conviction. On the passage, he","o":["is disqualified only after completing the sentence","remains qualified until the appeal is decided","is disqualified only on a decision of the Speaker","stands disqualified from the date of conviction"],"a":3,"e":"Section 8(3) operates from conviction; Lily Thomas removed the pending-appeal protection, and only a stay of the conviction itself avoids disqualification.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Electoral Bonds Scheme was struck down in 2024 as violating","o":["the donors' right to privacy under Article 21","the right of adult suffrage under Article 326","the powers of the Election Commission under Article 324","the voter's right to information under Article 19(1)(a)"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the Court found a violation of the voter's right to information under Article 19(1)(a).","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"How does the 2023 Act differ from the arrangement laid down in Anoop Baranwal?","o":["It removes the Leader of the Opposition from the selection committee altogether","It makes the CEC removable at the pleasure of the President of India","It vests the power of appointment in Parliament acting by resolution","It substitutes a Union Cabinet Minister for the Chief Justice on the selection committee"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the Chief Justice was replaced with a Cabinet Minister nominated by the Prime Minister.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P09","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law / Tax","t":"Mineral Rights, Royalty and Fiscal Federalism","x":"Entry 54 of the Union List permits Parliament to regulate mines and mineral development to the extent that Parliament declares it expedient in the public interest, while Entry 50 of the State List empowers the States to tax mineral rights 'subject to any limitations imposed by Parliament by law relating to mineral development'. Entry 49 permits States to tax lands and buildings. In India Cement Ltd v. State of Tamil Nadu (1990), a seven-judge Bench held that royalty on minerals is a tax, and that a State cess on royalty was therefore beyond the competence of the State because it amounted to a tax on a tax which the State could not impose. A clerical error later suggested that royalty was not a tax, and the controversy was referred to a nine-judge Bench. In Mineral Area Development Authority v. Steel Authority of India (July 2024), the Bench by a majority of eight to one held that royalty is a contractual payment made to the lessor for the right to extract minerals and not a tax. It further held that Parliament's power under Entry 54 does not include the power to tax mineral rights, which falls to the States under Entry 50, though Parliament may impose limitations by legislation on mineral development, and that the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 does not by itself limit that power. The dissenting judge would have held that allowing States to tax minerals would disturb the national mineral policy. In August 2024, the Court allowed States to recover the past dues from 1 April 2005, to be paid in instalments over twelve years, without interest or penalty. Separately, in Mohit Minerals v. Union of India (2022), the Court held that recommendations of the GST Council are not binding on the Union and States, being only persuasive, since Article 246A confers concurrent power on both to legislate on GST, and cooperative federalism requires dialogue and not dominance.","q":[{"q":"According to the passage, in the 2024 decision, royalty on minerals was held to be","o":["a contractual payment made to the lessor and not a tax","an exempt category of income","a tax within the meaning of Entry 50","a cess imposed by Parliament"],"a":0,"e":"The majority held royalty to be a contractual payment for the right to extract minerals.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following correctly describes the effect of the 2024 decision on the power of the States?","o":["States may not levy any tax on minerals","States may tax mineral rights under Entry 50, subject to limitations Parliament may impose by law relating to mineral development","Only Parliament may tax mineral rights under Entry 54","States may tax minerals only after Presidential consent"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that Entry 50 is the source of the States' power, which is subject to Parliament's limits.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following decisions is overruled by the 2024 judgment on the point of royalty being a tax?","o":["Mohit Minerals v. Union of India","India Cement Ltd v. State of Tamil Nadu","Steel Authority of India v. State","None of these"],"a":1,"e":"India Cement had held royalty to be a tax; the nine-judge Bench held otherwise.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The arrangement allowed by the Court for the recovery of past dues from the States' tax was","o":["No recovery at all","Recovery only for the future","Recovery from 1 April 2005 in instalments over twelve years without interest or penalty","Immediate full payment with interest"],"a":2,"e":"This is as described in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The Court in Mohit Minerals held that GST Council recommendations are","o":["binding only on the States","binding on both the Union and the States","persuasive only, in view of Article 246A and cooperative federalism","binding only on the Union"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states they are not binding but persuasive.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The dissenting judge's concern, as reflected in the passage, was that","o":["the case should have been decided by a smaller Bench","allowing States to tax minerals would undermine national mineral policy","royalty should be treated as a tax","India Cement was rightly overruled"],"a":1,"e":"The passage records the concern about disturbance of national mineral policy.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P10","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism: Delhi's Services, Article 356 and Judicial Review","x":"Article 239AA, inserted in 1991, gives the National Capital Territory of Delhi a Legislative Assembly and Council of Ministers, while providing that the Lieutenant Governor (LG) administers the territory. The Assembly may legislate on matters in the State and Concurrent Lists, except Entries 1 (public order), 2 (police) and 18 (land) of the State List. In NCT of Delhi v. Union of India (2018), a Constitution Bench held that the LG is bound by the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers in matters within the Assembly's legislative competence, and may refer a matter to the President only in exceptional cases of substantial difference, not as a routine. In NCT of Delhi v. Union of India (2023), another Constitution Bench held that the Delhi Government has legislative and executive control over 'services', that is, Entry 41 of the State List, except in respect of public order, police and land. The Court said that a democratically elected government must have control over the officers who implement its policies, and that the principle of collective responsibility depends on such control. Parliament then passed the Government of National Capital Territory of Delhi (Amendment) Act, 2023, which created the National Capital Civil Service Authority, chaired by the Chief Minister but with two senior officials, and empowered the LG to decide in the case of difference. The challenge to this Act was referred to a larger Bench, where the argument turns on whether Parliament may, under Article 239AA(7), legislate to alter a constitutional position declared by the Court, as distinct from removing its legal basis. Federalism has also been guarded through judicial review of the use of Article 356. In S.R. Bommai v. Union of India (1994), a nine-judge Bench held that a Presidential Proclamation is subject to judicial review, at least for malafides, irrelevant considerations or absence of material, that a majority must be tested on the floor of the House and not by the Governor's subjective view, that the Assembly should not be dissolved before Parliament approves the Proclamation, and that federalism and secularism are part of the basic structure.","q":[{"q":"According to the 2023 Constitution Bench decision, the Delhi Government's control over 'services' does not extend to","o":["education and health officers","every area of administration","Delhi's planning department","public order, police and land"],"a":3,"e":"The Bench excepted the three matters outside the Assembly's competence.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In the 2018 decision, the Court held that the LG may refer a matter to the President","o":["on every difference of opinion","only for matters of police","only in exceptional cases of substantial difference, not as a matter of routine","never"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following reasons for control over services by an elected government is given in the passage?","o":["Collective responsibility depends on the government's control over those who implement its policies","It is required by Article 356","It would increase the Union's power","It is required by Entry 18"],"a":0,"e":"The Court linked democracy and collective responsibility with control over officers.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The question referred to the larger Bench regarding the 2023 Amendment Act is essentially whether Parliament","o":["must obtain the Assembly's consent","can alter, by legislation under Article 239AA(7), a constitutional position declared by the Court, as distinct from removing its legal basis","can abolish the Assembly by ordinary law","can legislate at all for Delhi"],"a":1,"e":"This is the problem presented in the passage.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a holding in S.R. Bommai?","o":["A Presidential Proclamation under Article 356 is subject to judicial review","The majority of a Government is to be tested on the floor of the House","Federalism and secularism form part of the basic structure","The Assembly may be dissolved at once, before the Proclamation is approved"],"a":3,"e":"Bommai held that the Assembly should not be dissolved before parliamentary approval.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Assertion (A): After Bommai, the Governor's subjective report alone is not sufficient to oust an elected State Government. Reason (R): The Court held that a Government's majority must be tested on the floor of the House. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true, and R explains A","A is true but R is false"],"a":2,"e":"The floor test requirement displaces the Governor's subjective view.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P11","s":"Environmental Law","sf":"Environmental Law / International Law","t":"Climate Law: The Paris Agreement and India's Domestic Architecture","x":"Article 2(1)(a) of the Paris Agreement (2015) aims to hold the increase in global average temperature well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees. Under Article 4, each Party must prepare, communicate and maintain successive nationally determined contributions (NDCs) reflecting progression over time. The Agreement is to be implemented to reflect equity and the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities and respective capabilities (CBDR-RC), in the light of different national circumstances, which means that all States share responsibility but the obligations differ according to historical contribution and capacity. Article 6 allows voluntary cooperative approaches, including the use of internationally transferred mitigation outcomes, and establishes a mechanism for trading emission reductions. At the Glasgow summit in 2021, India announced the goal of net-zero emissions by 2070. India's updated NDC (2022) commits to reducing the emissions intensity of its GDP by 45 per cent by 2030 from the 2005 level, and to achieving about 50 per cent cumulative electric power installed capacity from non-fossil sources by 2030, subject to technology transfer and low-cost climate finance. India has no single comprehensive climate statute; its response rests on sectoral laws such as the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and the Energy Conservation Act, 2001. The Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act, 2022 empowers the Centre to specify a carbon credit trading scheme and to require designated consumers to use a minimum share of non-fossil energy. The Green Credit Programme (2023), notified under the 1986 Act, is a voluntary, market-based mechanism that rewards environmentally positive actions such as afforestation and water conservation. In M.K. Ranjitsinh v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court read Articles 14 and 21 to include a right to be free from the adverse effects of climate change, giving a constitutional dimension to climate protection in the absence of a general statute.","q":[{"q":"The temperature goal in Article 2(1)(a) of the Paris Agreement is to hold the increase well below","o":["2 degrees Celsius, while pursuing efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees","3 degrees Celsius, with no further effort","1 degree Celsius above the 2005 level","2.5 degrees Celsius, with 2 degrees as a stretch target"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the goal in these terms.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The principle of CBDR-RC means that","o":["all States share responsibility for climate change, but their obligations differ according to historical contribution and capacity","developing States are exempt from submitting NDCs","only developed States have obligations under the Agreement","all States must reduce emissions by an identical percentage"],"a":0,"e":"The passage explains the principle in these terms.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes India's domestic climate architecture on the basis of the passage?","o":["India has no single comprehensive climate statute and relies on sectoral laws such as the 1986 Act and the Energy Conservation Act, as amended","India's NDC is a statute enforceable in the High Courts","India's climate response is codified wholly in the Energy Conservation Act, 2001","India has enacted a Climate Change Act that creates a mandatory national carbon tax"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that there is no single comprehensive climate statute.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The use of internationally transferred mitigation outcomes is provided for in","o":["Section 3 of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986","the Green Credit Programme of 2023","Article 6 of the Paris Agreement","Article 2(1)(a) of the Paris Agreement"],"a":2,"e":"Article 6 provides for voluntary cooperative approaches, including transferred mitigation outcomes.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Green Credit Programme, 2023 is best described as","o":["a treaty obligation under Article 6 of the Paris Agreement","a voluntary, market-based mechanism rewarding environmentally positive actions such as afforestation and water conservation","a replacement for the carbon credit trading scheme under the Energy Conservation Act","a compulsory tax on all industrial emissions"],"a":1,"e":"The passage describes it as voluntary and market-based.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Assertion (A): The decision in M.K. Ranjitsinh gives climate protection a constitutional footing in India even though there is no comprehensive climate statute. Reason (R): The Court read Articles 14 and 21 to include a right to be free from the adverse effects of climate change. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A"],"a":3,"e":"R states the basis for the constitutional recognition asserted in A.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P12","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Directive Principles, Article 31C and the Preamble: 25th and 42nd Amendments","x":"Part IV of the Constitution contains the Directive Principles, which are not enforceable by courts. In State of Madras v. Champakam Dorairajan (1951), the Court held that Fundamental Rights prevail over Directive Principles, which must conform to and run as subsidiary to them. The Twenty-fifth Amendment (1971) inserted Article 31C, protecting laws giving effect to the policy in Article 39(b) and (c) from challenge for violation of Articles 14, 19 and 31, and providing that no law containing a declaration that it gives effect to such policy could be questioned in any court on the ground that it does not do so. In Kesavananda Bharati (1973), the second part of that protection was struck down, so that courts may examine whether a law actually gives effect to the policy of Article 39(b) or (c). The Forty-second Amendment (1976) extended Article 31C to laws giving effect to any Directive Principle in Part IV. In Minerva Mills (1980), the Court struck down this extension, holding that harmony and balance between Parts III and IV is part of the basic structure. The 42nd Amendment also inserted Articles 39A (equal justice and free legal aid), 43A (workers' participation in management) and 48A (protection of the environment), and added Part IVA with Article 51A on Fundamental Duties. It inserted the words 'socialist', 'secular' and 'integrity' in the Preamble. Article 39(b) provides that the ownership and control of the material resources of the community should be so distributed as best to subserve the common good. In Property Owners Association v. State of Maharashtra (2024), a nine-judge Bench held that not every privately owned resource is a 'material resource of the community'; some may qualify depending on factors such as the nature of the resource, its impact on community well-being, scarcity and the consequences of its concentration in private hands.","q":[{"q":"Article 39A, providing for equal justice and free legal aid, was inserted by the","o":["Forty-fourth Amendment","Twenty-fifth Amendment","Eighty-sixth Amendment","Forty-second Amendment"],"a":3,"e":"The passage lists Article 39A among the Articles inserted by the 42nd Amendment.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following words was NOT inserted into the Preamble by the 42nd Amendment?","o":["Integrity","Secular","Democratic","Socialist"],"a":2,"e":"The 42nd Amendment inserted 'socialist', 'secular' and 'integrity'. 'Democratic' formed part of the original Preamble.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A State law gives effect to Article 48A, and in doing so violates Article 14. The law claims immunity under Article 31C. After Minerva Mills, the claim","o":["succeeds, since Article 48A was itself inserted by the 42nd Amendment","fails, since Article 31C protects only laws giving effect to Article 39(b) and (c)","succeeds only if the law contains a declaration that it gives effect to Article 48A","succeeds, since the 42nd Amendment extended Article 31C to all Directive Principles"],"a":1,"e":"Minerva Mills struck down the extension of Article 31C beyond Article 39(b) and (c).","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A statute declares that it gives effect to Article 39(c), but in substance it concentrates wealth and violates Article 14. A court","o":["must uphold the law, since the declaration of its purpose is conclusive on courts","must uphold the law if it was passed by a two-thirds majority in Parliament","may test whether it truly gives effect to Article 39(b) or (c), and deny protection if not","cannot examine the matter at all, since Article 31C bars all judicial review"],"a":2,"e":"After Kesavananda, courts may examine whether a law actually gives effect to the policy; the declaration is not conclusive.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The principle laid down in Champakam Dorairajan is that","o":["Directive Principles are enforceable by courts","Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles are equal in rank","Fundamental Rights prevail over Directive Principles","Directive Principles prevail over Fundamental Rights"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that Directive Principles must conform to and run as subsidiary to Fundamental Rights.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 2024 decision in Property Owners Association v. State of Maharashtra held that","o":["no privately owned resource can ever fall within the scope of Article 39(b)","Article 39(b) applies only to resources owned by the Government or its agencies","not every privately owned resource qualifies, though some may","all privately owned resources can be redistributed under Article 39(b)"],"a":2,"e":"The Bench rejected both extremes and held that qualification depends on factors such as nature, impact, scarcity and concentration.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P13","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"The Tenth Schedule and the Speaker's Delay","x":"The Tenth Schedule, inserted in 1985, provides for the disqualification of a member of Parliament or a State Legislature who voluntarily gives up the membership of his party or defies its whip on a vote. The Schedule recognises a merger only where not less than two-thirds of the legislature party agree to it. Under paragraph 6, the question of disqualification is to be decided by the Speaker or Chairman of the House. In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), a Constitution Bench upheld the Schedule but struck down paragraph 7, which barred the jurisdiction of courts, and held that the Speaker, in exercising this power, acts as a tribunal whose decisions are subject to limited judicial review, for example for mala fides, perversity or violation of constitutional mandates. Experience has shown that Speakers, belonging to the ruling party in most cases, have sometimes sat on disqualification petitions until the term of the House expires, rendering them infructuous. In Keisham Meghachandra Singh v. Hon'ble Speaker, Manipur Legislative Assembly (2020), the Supreme Court held that Speakers should ordinarily decide such petitions within three months and urged Parliament to consider vesting the power in an independent tribunal. In July 2025, hearing petitions concerning legislators in Telangana who had defected after being elected on the ticket of one party, the Court directed the Speaker to decide the petitions within three months, observing that the Speaker is not immune from judicial scrutiny and that allowing petitions to be delayed until the term of the Assembly lapses would defeat the constitutional policy against defection. The Court added that Parliament should reconsider whether the Speaker, who remains a member of a political party, is the appropriate authority for adjudicating such disputes.","q":[{"q":"The ground on which paragraph 7 of the Tenth Schedule was struck down in Kihoto Hollohan was that it","o":["excluded the jurisdiction of courts","provided for disqualification without a hearing","made the Speaker's decision appealable to the President","permitted mergers without a two-thirds majority"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that paragraph 7 barred judicial jurisdiction and was struck down.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In the passage, the Speaker deciding a disqualification petition is described as","o":["a purely legislative authority immune from review","an executive officer of the Government","a tribunal whose decision is subject to limited judicial review","an appellate authority over the High Court"],"a":2,"e":"Kihoto Hollohan held that the Speaker acts as a tribunal whose decisions can be reviewed on limited grounds.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following best explains why a delay by the Speaker is considered constitutionally problematic?","o":["It transfers jurisdiction to the High Court","It increases the number of petitions","It may render the petition infructuous when the House's term expires, thereby defeating the anti-defection policy","It permits mergers with less than two-thirds support"],"a":2,"e":"The passage identifies infructuousness through delay as defeating the constitutional policy.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A group of 40% of a legislature party joins another party and claims that it constitutes a 'merger'. On the passage, the claim is","o":["valid if the Speaker approves","invalid only if the whip is defied","invalid, since a merger requires the agreement of at least two-thirds of the legislature party","valid, since a merger requires a simple majority"],"a":2,"e":"The Schedule recognises a merger only with at least two-thirds of the legislature party.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"What was the direction of the Supreme Court in the July 2025 Telangana matter?","o":["The petitions were transferred to the Election Commission","The matter was referred to a tribunal created by the Court","The Speaker was directed to disqualify the legislators","The Speaker was directed to decide the petitions within three months"],"a":3,"e":"The Court directed a decision within three months and observed that the Speaker is not immune from scrutiny.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which proposition is the most consistent with the passage?","o":["Parliament is bound to constitute an independent tribunal","Courts may fix a reasonable time for the Speaker to decide, although the Schedule itself prescribes none, because inaction can defeat constitutional policy","Judicial review of a Speaker's decision is barred in all cases","The Speaker's decision on disqualification is final under paragraph 7"],"a":1,"e":"The Court, while leaving the Speaker to decide, laid down a time frame by reason of the risk of defeat of the anti-defection policy; the Court only urged Parliament to reconsider the authority.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P14","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Amending Power and the Basic Structure: A Doctrinal Ladder","x":"Article 368 confers on Parliament the power to amend the Constitution. In Shankari Prasad (1951) and Sajjan Singh (1965), the Supreme Court held that an amendment under Article 368 is not 'law' within Article 13(2), and that fundamental rights could therefore be amended. In I.C. Golaknath v. State of Punjab (1967), by a majority of six to five, the Court overruled these decisions, held that fundamental rights are 'transcendental' and cannot be abridged by amendment, and invoked prospective overruling to save past amendments. Parliament responded with the 24th Amendment (1971), which declared that Article 13 does not apply to constitutional amendments, and the 25th Amendment. In Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973), a thirteen-judge Bench, by a narrow majority of seven to six, upheld the 24th Amendment but held that the power to amend does not include the power to alter the 'basic structure or framework' of the Constitution; the judges differed in listing the features of the basic structure. In Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975), the Court applied the doctrine to strike down a clause of the 39th Amendment that exempted the election of the Prime Minister from judicial scrutiny, as violating free and fair elections, the rule of law and judicial review. After the 42nd Amendment, which sought to exclude judicial review of amendments and to give primacy to the Directive Principles, the Court in Minerva Mills v. Union of India (1980) struck down these provisions, holding that the limited nature of the amending power is itself a basic feature, and that the harmony between Parts III and IV is part of that structure. In I.R. Coelho v. State of Tamil Nadu (2007), a nine-judge Bench held that laws placed in the Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973, the date of Kesavananda, are open to challenge on the ground that they violate fundamental rights which form part of the basic structure. In Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (2015), the Court struck down the 99th Amendment and the National Judicial Appointments Commission Act, holding that judicial independence, and thereby the primacy of the judiciary in appointments, is part of the basic structure.","q":[{"q":"The Court in Golaknath employed the doctrine of prospective overruling in order to","o":["apply its decision retrospectively to all amendments","strike down the 24th Amendment","overrule Kesavananda","save past amendments from invalidation while holding that fundamental rights cannot in future be abridged"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the Court saved past amendments by prospective overruling.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following correctly states the effect of the 24th Amendment as upheld in Kesavananda?","o":["Amendments are not 'law' under Article 13, but the power does not extend to altering the basic structure","Fundamental rights are absolutely unamendable","The Court lost the power of judicial review","Parliament may amend any part of the Constitution without limit"],"a":0,"e":"The 24th Amendment was upheld, subject to the basic structure limitation.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain, the clause of the 39th Amendment was struck down on the ground that it","o":["violated Article 21 only","violated free and fair elections, the rule of law and judicial review as parts of the basic structure","was not passed by the requisite majority","affected the federal structure"],"a":1,"e":"The passage lists the three grounds.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The holding in Minerva Mills that 'the limited nature of the amending power is itself a basic feature' means that","o":["fundamental rights prevail over Directive Principles in every case","Parliament cannot, by amending Article 368, enlarge its own amending power into an unlimited one","the Constitution is unamendable","Parliament cannot amend Article 368 at all"],"a":1,"e":"Parliament cannot use its limited power to remove the limits on that power.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A law was placed in the Ninth Schedule on 1 January 1980. On the basis of I.R. Coelho, the law","o":["may be challenged for violation of fundamental rights forming part of the basic structure","can be challenged only before the President","is immune from all judicial review","is void because the Schedule is unconstitutional"],"a":0,"e":"Laws placed after 24 April 1973 are open to challenge.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following pairings of case and principle is INCORRECT?","o":["I.R. Coelho - Ninth Schedule laws are wholly immune after Kesavananda","Minerva Mills - harmony of Parts III and IV is part of the basic structure","Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association (2015) - judicial independence is part of the basic structure","Golaknath - fundamental rights are not amendable (later overruled)"],"a":0,"e":"Coelho held that Ninth Schedule laws after 24 April 1973 are subject to review.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P15","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Natural Resources, Federalism and the Constitutional Framework for the Environment","x":"Environmental governance in India rests on a constitutional framework shaped by the 42nd Amendment (1976), which inserted Article 48A, directing the State to protect and improve the environment and to safeguard forests and wildlife, and Article 51A(g), making the protection of the environment a fundamental duty. That Amendment also moved forests and the protection of wild animals and birds to the Concurrent List (Entries 17A and 17B). Public health and sanitation, agriculture, water and land are State subjects, industries and mines are in the Union List subject to the limits of the relevant entries, and Entry 56 of the Union List allows Parliament to regulate inter-State rivers where it declares it expedient. Article 253 empowers Parliament to legislate for the whole or any part of India to implement an international treaty, agreement or decision, and the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 was enacted under it to give effect to the decisions of the 1972 Stockholm Conference. Article 39(b) directs the State to secure that the ownership and control of the material resources of the community are so distributed as best to subserve the common good. In Property Owners Association v. State of Maharashtra (November 2024), a nine-judge Bench held, by majority, that not every privately owned resource is a 'material resource of the community' under Article 39(b), though some may be, depending on factors such as the nature of the resource, its impact on the well-being of the community, its scarcity and the consequences of its being held in private hands. In Re: Natural Resources Allocation (Special Reference No. 1 of 2012), the Court held that auction is not a constitutional requirement for every alienation of natural resources, the method depending on the nature of the resource and the object of the policy, but that any method must be fair, transparent and consistent with the public trust. In Manohar Lal Sharma v. Principal Secretary (2014), the Court held the allocation of coal blocks arbitrary and illegal and cancelled it, with a few exceptions.","q":[{"q":"Parliament is empowered by Article 253 to","o":["legislate for the whole or any part of India to implement an international treaty, agreement or decision","legislate only on matters in the Concurrent List","legislate on any State subject at any time","override the Constitution in order to implement a treaty"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the scope of Article 253.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The 42nd Amendment moved which of the following subjects to the Concurrent List?","o":["Mines and minerals","Public health and sanitation","Forests and the protection of wild animals and birds","Water and irrigation"],"a":2,"e":"The passage identifies Entries 17A and 17B.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Property Owners Association (2024), the nine-judge Bench held that","o":["no privately owned resource can ever be a material resource of the community","not every privately owned resource is a material resource of the community, though some may be, depending on specified factors","Article 39(b) is unenforceable by the State","all privately owned resources are material resources of the community"],"a":1,"e":"The passage records the holding and the factors.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to the Special Reference of 2012, as described, the alienation of natural resources","o":["may be by any method, however arbitrary","need not always be by auction, but the method chosen must be fair, transparent and consistent with the public trust","must always be by auction","is a matter wholly outside judicial review"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that auction is not constitutionally mandatory for every resource.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A State enacts a law acquiring every privately owned mine within its territory, on the single premise that all private property is a material resource of the community under Article 39(b). In the light of the 2024 decision, the law is","o":["invalid only because the State has not obtained the approval of the President","unassailable, since Article 39(b) applies to all property","open to challenge, since the premise is untenable and each resource must be tested against factors such as nature, scarcity and impact on the community","valid because mines are in the Union List"],"a":2,"e":"The 2024 decision rejects the premise that all private resources fall within Article 39(b).","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Assertion (A): Parliament was competent to enact the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 although the environment is not separately listed as a legislative subject. Reason (R): Article 253 empowers Parliament to legislate to implement international decisions, and the Act gave effect to decisions of the 1972 Stockholm Conference. Which is correct?","o":["A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A"],"a":2,"e":"R supplies the source of legislative power for the 1986 Act.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P16","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 368(2): Ratification, the 52nd Amendment and the 97th Amendment","x":"Article 368(2) provides that a Bill to amend the Constitution may be introduced only in either House of Parliament and must be passed in each House by a majority of the total membership and by not less than two-thirds of the members present and voting; the President must then give assent. A proviso adds that where the amendment seeks to change certain federal provisions, it must also be ratified by the legislatures of not less than one-half of the States. These provisions include the election of the President (Articles 54 and 55), the extent of the executive power of the Union and the States, the Supreme Court and the High Courts, the distribution of legislative powers between the Union and the States, any of the Lists in the Seventh Schedule, the representation of States in Parliament, and Article 368 itself. In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), the Court held, by a majority of 3:2, that paragraph 7 of the Tenth Schedule, inserted by the Fifty-second Amendment and barring the jurisdiction of courts over disqualification decisions, affected the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court and High Courts and therefore needed ratification, which had not been obtained. Paragraph 7 was held invalid, but being severable, the rest of the Tenth Schedule survived. The Constitution (Ninety-seventh Amendment) Act, 2011 added 'co-operative societies' to Article 19(1)(c), inserted Article 43B, and added a new Part IXB (Articles 243ZH to 243ZT) on co-operative societies. Since 'co-operative societies' is a subject in the State List, the amendment needed ratification, which was not obtained. In Union of India v. Rajendra N. Shah (2021), the Supreme Court, by a majority of 2:1, struck down Part IXB insofar as it applied to co-operative societies in the States, but upheld it for multi-State co-operative societies, which fall within Parliament's domain.","q":[{"q":"An amendment affecting the Lists in the Seventh Schedule requires ratification by","o":["the legislatures of at least half of the States","the Rajya Sabha acting alone by a special majority","the legislatures of all the States without exception","the legislatures of two-thirds of the States"],"a":0,"e":"The proviso to Article 368(2) requires ratification by not less than one-half of the State legislatures.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following amendments would require ratification by State legislatures under the proviso to Article 368(2)?","o":["One altering the Lists in the Seventh Schedule","One lowering the voting age under Article 326","One adding a new Fundamental Duty to Article 51A","One altering a Directive Principle in Part IV"],"a":0,"e":"The proviso covers changes to the Seventh Schedule Lists; the other subjects are not listed in it.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Kihoto Hollohan, paragraph 7 of the Tenth Schedule was held invalid because","o":["it violated the Fundamental Right to equality under Article 14 of the Constitution","it was passed by less than two-thirds of the members present and voting","it ousted court jurisdiction without ratification by the States","it imposed an unreasonable restriction on the freedoms under Article 19(1)"],"a":2,"e":"The passage gives the reason: it ousted court jurisdiction, requiring ratification, which was not obtained.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A challenge is made to the election of the board of a multi-State co-operative society under Part IXB. After Rajendra N. Shah, Part IXB","o":["continues to apply to multi-State co-operative societies","applies only to co-operative societies in the States","applies only if the States ratify it","no longer applies to any co-operative society"],"a":0,"e":"The Court struck down Part IXB for State societies but upheld it for multi-State co-operative societies.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the procedure under Article 368(2) as described?","o":["A Bill may also be introduced in a State Legislature and then sent on to Parliament","A Bill needs only a simple majority in each House if the President is willing to assent","If the Rajya Sabha disagrees, the Bill may be passed at a joint sitting of both Houses","Introduction in either House, then a majority of the total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting"],"a":3,"e":"Article 368(2) requires introduction in Parliament and the special majority in each House, followed by assent. No joint sitting is provided.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Court's treatment of paragraph 7 in Kihoto Hollohan illustrates that","o":["the Tenth Schedule as a whole was held unconstitutional by the Court","a defect in one severable provision need not invalidate the whole amendment","ratification is required for every amendment affecting Fundamental Rights","the whole of the Fifty-second Amendment fell for want of ratification"],"a":1,"e":"Paragraph 7 was severed and struck down; the rest of the Tenth Schedule survived.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P17","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence","t":"Kelsen's Pure Theory, Revolutions and Legal Realism","x":"Hans Kelsen's Pure Theory of Law seeks a science of law purged of political, moral and sociological elements. For Kelsen, law is a hierarchy of norms in which the validity of every norm derives from a higher norm, until the chain terminates in the Grundnorm, a hypothetical, presupposed basic norm, which is not itself posited by any authority and whose validity is assumed as a juristic hypothesis. The Grundnorm does not state what is just, but only that one ought to behave as the framers of the first constitution have ordained. A legal system must also be, by and large, efficacious, for a norm that is not effective loses its validity. Kelsen's theory was invoked in State v. Dosso (1958), where the Supreme Court of Pakistan, after a military coup, held that a successful revolution is an internationally recognised method of changing a constitution and becomes a law-creating fact; the new regime's order was thereby validated. In Asma Jilani v. Government of the Punjab (1972), the same Court rejected Dosso, holding that the Grundnorm cannot be located in an efficacious usurpation, and that Kelsen's theory had been misapplied. Legal realists, in contrast, were sceptical of rules. Oliver Wendell Holmes defined law as a prediction of what the courts will do in fact, from the viewpoint of the 'bad man' who cares only for the material consequences of his conduct. Jerome Frank, in the strand of 'fact scepticism', stressed that the uncertainty of facts found by the trial court makes the prediction of judgment unreliable, while Karl Llewellyn emphasised the 'law-in-action' over the 'law-in-books'. Hart criticised the realists for 'rule scepticism', arguing that predicting what a judge will do ignores the internal point of view from which the rule is accepted as a standard, and that the predictive account cannot explain why courts regard themselves as bound by rules.","q":[{"q":"The Grundnorm, according to Kelsen, is","o":["a moral principle of natural law","a command of the sovereign","a statute enacted by the first legislature","a hypothetical, presupposed norm which is the final source of validity of the legal order"],"a":3,"e":"The passage describes it as hypothetical and presupposed, not posited.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Dosso, the Supreme Court of Pakistan treated a successful revolution as","o":["a law-creating fact which validates the new regime's order","an illegal act that cannot be validated","irrelevant to the Grundnorm","a matter for the legislature alone"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records this holding.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following best states the reason for the Court's rejection of Dosso in Asma Jilani?","o":["Kelsen's theory is wholly wrong","The coup was not successful","The coup was approved by the legislature","The Grundnorm cannot be located in an efficacious usurpation, and Kelsen's theory was misapplied"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states the reasoning.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Holmes's 'bad man' theory, the law is","o":["a body of divine commands","a prediction of what the courts will do in fact, as it concerns the material consequences of conduct","a moral ideal","a system of rights independent of courts"],"a":1,"e":"The passage sets out Holmes's predictive definition.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Hart's criticism of the realists is that their account","o":["is too concerned with rules","accepts the command theory","fails to account for the internal point of view from which officials accept rules as standards and treat themselves as bound","gives too much weight to morality"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that the predictive account ignores the internal point of view.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following pairings is INCORRECT based on the passage?","o":["Llewellyn - law-in-action","Kelsen - hierarchy of norms","Holmes - Grundnorm as the source of validity","Frank - fact scepticism"],"a":2,"e":"Holmes is associated with the predictive theory; the Grundnorm is Kelsen's concept.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P18","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence / Constitutional Law","t":"Precedent, Ratio Decidendi and the Authority of the Supreme Court","x":"Article 141 of the Constitution provides that the law declared by the Supreme Court shall be binding on all courts within the territory of India. The expression 'law declared' is taken to refer to the ratio decidendi of a decision, that is, the principle of law necessary for the decision on the material facts, and not to obiter dicta, which are observations made in passing; though the obiter dicta of the Supreme Court are entitled to great weight, they are not binding in the strict sense. Jurisprudence has differed on the nature of judicial decision. The declaratory theory, associated with Blackstone, holds that judges do not make law but discover and declare what the law already is. The opposing view, associated with Bentham and Austin, regards judge-made law as law made by the judges, and Austin called the common law 'judiciary law'. A decision is said to be per incuriam, and therefore not binding, where it is given in ignorance of a binding statute or of a binding authority of a superior court, as explained in Young v. Bristol Aeroplane Co. and applied in A.R. Antulay v. R.S. Nayak (1988). A decision rendered sub silentio, without the point having been argued or considered, has no precedential value on that point. The doctrine of stare decisis promotes certainty, but the Supreme Court is not bound by its own decisions and may overrule them, for example in Golaknath, where it applied prospective overruling to avoid disruption. In Central Board of Dawoodi Bohra Community v. State of Maharashtra (2005), the Court held that a Bench of coequal strength is bound by the decision of another Bench of the same strength, and that if it disagrees, it can only refer the matter to a larger Bench, which also applies to decisions of Constitution Benches, which a smaller Bench cannot doubt. Under the doctrine of merger, as explained in Kunhayammed v. State of Kerala (2000), when a superior court entertains an appeal and passes a decree, the decree of the lower court merges in that of the superior court. The Court's power under Article 142 to do complete justice is not a licence to override statutory or constitutional prohibitions, and a curative petition, evolved in Rupa Ashok Hurra v. Ashok Hurra (2002), permits a final judgment to be reconsidered in rare cases of a gross abuse of process or violation of natural justice, where a review has been dismissed.","q":[{"q":"The 'law declared' within Article 141 refers to","o":["only the final order","every observation in a judgment","obiter dicta alone","the ratio decidendi of a decision"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that it refers to the ratio decidendi.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A decision given in ignorance of a binding statutory provision is","o":["valid for lower courts only","per incuriam, and not binding","binding as a precedent","a decision sub silentio only"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states this.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The declaratory theory of Blackstone holds that","o":["judges discover and declare the existing law","judges make new law","the legislature is subordinate to judges","precedent has no value"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states this.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A two-judge Bench disagrees with a decision of a Constitution Bench. In accordance with Dawoodi Bohra, it should","o":["refer the matter to a larger Bench, since a smaller Bench cannot doubt a Constitution Bench","overrule the Constitution Bench","decide as it prefers","ignore the decision"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that the smaller bench can only refer the matter to a larger Bench.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A curative petition is permitted","o":["only in criminal cases","as a second appeal","whenever a party loses","in rare cases of gross abuse of process or violation of natural justice, after the dismissal of a review"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states the position.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. Obiter dicta of the Supreme Court are binding in the same manner as ratio. 2. A decision sub silentio has no precedential value on the point not argued. 3. The Supreme Court may overrule its own earlier decisions. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"Obiter dicta are weighty but not strictly binding; statements 2 and 3 are accurate.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P19","s":"Environmental Law","sf":"Environmental Law","t":"Sustainable Development: Balance, Burden and Non-Regression","x":"The Brundtland Commission defined sustainable development as development that meets present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. The non-binding Rio Declaration of 1992 elaborates the idea: Principle 3 requires that the right to development be fulfilled so as to meet equitably the developmental and environmental needs of present and future generations, Principle 4 makes environmental protection an integral part of development, Principle 15 states the precautionary approach, and Principle 16 says that the polluter should, in principle, bear the cost of pollution. Indian courts, drawing on Articles 21, 48A and 51A(g), have made these ideas part of domestic law. In Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum (1996), the precautionary and polluter pays principles were accepted as essential features of sustainable development. In A.P. Pollution Control Board v. M.V. Nayudu (1999), the Court held that, where the effects of an activity are uncertain, the burden of showing that it is environmentally benign lies on its proponent, and stressed the need for expert environmental authorities. In Narmada Bachao Andolan v. Union of India (2000), the majority declined to halt the Sardar Sarovar project, holding that development and environment must be balanced and that a project may proceed where adequate environmental and rehabilitation safeguards are in place. In Intellectuals Forum, Tirupathi v. State of A.P. (2006), the Court invoked intergenerational equity and the public trust to protect water bodies. In Hanuman Laxman Aroskar v. Union of India (2019), the Court set aside an environmental clearance for an airport in Goa for want of a cumulative impact assessment and recognised the principle of non-regression: standards of environmental protection, once attained, should not be diluted without compelling justification. In Harbinder Singh Sekhon v. State of Punjab (February 2026), the Court held that a dilution of preventive environmental safeguards, without proportionate and scientifically substantiated justification, is arbitrary and infringes the right to a clean environment under Article 21.","q":[{"q":"The 'principle of non-regression' recognised in Aroskar means that","o":["standards of environmental protection once attained should not be diluted without compelling justification","developing States may not reduce their emissions","polluters are exempt from liability after a clearance","a project once cleared can never be reviewed"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the principle in these terms.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A State amends its rules to halve the mandatory buffer zone around industrial units, without any scientific study, to attract investment. In the light of the passage, the amendment is","o":["valid, since the right to development always prevails","open to challenge as arbitrary, since dilution of preventive safeguards needs proportionate, reasoned and scientific justification","invalid only if Parliament has passed a contrary law","valid, since the Rio Declaration is binding on the State"],"a":1,"e":"Harbinder Singh Sekhon (2026) condemns unjustified dilution as arbitrary and contrary to Article 21.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The approach of the majority in Narmada Bachao Andolan, as described in the passage, was that","o":["development always prevails over environmental concerns","development and environment must be balanced, and a project may proceed where adequate safeguards are in place","environmental concerns always prevail over development","courts must decide the technical merits of dam design"],"a":1,"e":"The majority accepted the project with safeguards, on a balancing approach.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Nayudu, where the effects of an activity on the environment are uncertain, the burden of proof is on","o":["the court","the citizen who objects to it","the pollution control board in every case","the person proposing the activity"],"a":3,"e":"The precautionary principle shifts the burden to the proponent.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Assertion (A): The principle of intergenerational equity restricts the freedom of the present generation in the use of natural resources. Reason (R): The Brundtland formulation requires present needs to be met without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true"],"a":2,"e":"R states the content of the principle that justifies the restriction in A.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Consider the following statements. 1. Principle 16 of the Rio Declaration states that the polluter should, in principle, bear the cost of pollution. 2. Principle 15 of the Rio Declaration states the precautionary approach. 3. The Rio Declaration is a binding treaty which Indian courts must enforce as such. Which of the statements are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statements 1 and 2 are accurate; the Declaration is non-binding and its principles operate in India through domestic law and judicial recognition.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P20","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"The 99th Amendment, the Collegium and Tribunals","x":"The appointment of judges has produced a sequence of decisions known as the Judges Cases. In S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (1981), the Court held that 'consultation' with the Chief Justice of India in Article 124(2) did not mean concurrence, giving the executive primacy. In Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (1993) (the Second Judges Case), a nine-judge Bench overruled this, holding that the CJI's opinion has primacy, formed through a collegium. In the Third Judges Case (Special Reference No. 1 of 1998, under Article 143), the Court held that the collegium for the Supreme Court consists of the CJI and four senior-most judges. The Constitution (Ninety-ninth Amendment) Act, 2014 inserted Articles 124A, 124B and 124C and set up the National Judicial Appointments Commission (NJAC) of six members: the CJI as chairperson, two senior-most Supreme Court judges, the Union Law Minister, and two eminent persons nominated by a committee of the Prime Minister, the CJI and the Leader of the Opposition in the Lok Sabha. In Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (2015) (the Fourth Judges Case), a Constitution Bench by 4:1 struck down the Amendment and the NJAC Act as violating the basic structure, namely the independence of the judiciary and the separation of powers, and revived the collegium. Justice Chelameswar dissented. Separately, the Forty-second Amendment inserted Articles 323A and 323B for administrative and other tribunals. In L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997), the Court struck down the provisions excluding the jurisdiction of the High Courts under Articles 226 and 227 and of the Supreme Court under Article 32, holding that judicial review is part of the basic structure; decisions of tribunals are subject to scrutiny before a Division Bench of the concerned High Court.","q":[{"q":"The decision which first held that the opinion of the Chief Justice of India has primacy in judicial appointments, formed through a collegium, is","o":["the Fourth Judges Case (2015)","the Second Judges Case (1993)","the Third Judges Case (1998)","S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (1981)"],"a":1,"e":"S.P. Gupta gave executive primacy; the Second Judges Case overruled it and gave primacy to the CJI.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The Ninety-ninth Amendment inserted which Articles to create the NJAC?","o":["Articles 143 and 144 relating to advisory jurisdiction","Articles 323A and 323B relating to tribunals","Articles 124A, 124B and 124C","Articles 217 and 222 relating to High Court judges"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that the 99th Amendment inserted Articles 124A, 124B and 124C.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following has NO role in nominating the two eminent persons to the NJAC?","o":["The Union Law Minister","The Chief Justice of India","The Prime Minister","The Leader of the Opposition in the Lok Sabha"],"a":0,"e":"The nominating committee is the Prime Minister, the CJI and the Leader of the Opposition. The Law Minister is an NJAC member but not on that committee.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In the Fourth Judges Case (2015), the Court","o":["struck down the NJAC Act but upheld the 99th Amendment as valid","struck down both by 4:1 as violating judicial independence and separation of powers","upheld the 99th Amendment and the NJAC Act by 4:1, with one judge dissenting","struck down the 99th Amendment and the NJAC Act unanimously, with no dissent"],"a":1,"e":"A Constitution Bench struck down both by 4:1, with Justice Chelameswar dissenting, and revived the collegium.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A litigant is aggrieved by an order of the Central Administrative Tribunal. Following L. Chandra Kumar as described, the proper first course is to approach","o":["the President of India","a parliamentary committee","the Supreme Court directly under Article 32","a Division Bench of the concerned High Court"],"a":3,"e":"Tribunal decisions are subject to scrutiny before a Division Bench of the concerned High Court.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"As per the Third Judges Case, the collegium for appointments to the Supreme Court consists of","o":["the CJI and all other judges of the Court","the CJI and two senior-most judges","the CJI alone","the CJI and four senior-most judges"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the collegium for the Supreme Court consists of the CJI and four senior-most judges.","d":"Tough"}]}]};
